::STAT_U01_L07_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
In item 1, A school survey records each student grade level and minutes spent reading each night. Which response best applies data types, displays, relative frequency, and choosing an appropriate representation to support a statistically valid conclusion for the lesson topic: 
{
~A. Select the graph or rule that looks most familiar without checking the variables. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~B. Use only the largest number in the data set as the conclusion. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
=C. Identify whether each variable is categorical or numerical before choosing the display. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
~D. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
For item 2, A club compares favorite activity category with number of meetings attended. What should a student do first to analyze the situation using data types, displays, relative frequency, and choosing an appropriate representation in the context of 
{
~A. Use only the largest number in the data set as the conclusion. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~B. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~C. Ignore the context and report only a formula or isolated calculation. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
=D. Choose a representation that matches the variable type and supports comparison. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Item 3 asks about Selecting, Constructing, and Justifying Data Displays. A teacher displays test scores from one class and participation categories from another class. Which interpretation is most appropriate when using 
{
=A. Use relative frequencies when comparing groups of different sizes. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
~B. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~C. Ignore the context and report only a formula or isolated calculation. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~D. Use the same method for every problem without checking conditions. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
}

